  "slug": "neuronal-activity-dependent-aa6a",
  "summary": "Six preprints link histone marks, cytosine modifications and allelic chromatin to neuronal activation, differentiation and transcript regulation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is epigenetic and regulatory-state control of gene expression, with most items centered on neural cells. In aged mouse dentate gyrus, an H3K9me3-high state formed via Suv39h1 blunts neuronal activation and synaptic remodeling, and reducing these neurons improves memory. During mouse ES cell neuronal differentiation, single-base profiling reveals a 5hmC-to-5mC transition at gene bodies and transcription end sites. In human iPSC neurogenesis, H19 isoform switching separates a pluripotency-associated non-canonical variant from canonical H19 and IGF2 expression. In human microglia, allele-specific open chromatin ties the AUD-associated variant rs7307261 to altered ETS-factor binding. TrES-seq adds joint scRNA and histone-mark profiling in human tonsil cells, and a cross-species adipose study addresses transposable elements in aging, extending the theme beyond neurons.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
